Write-up for PA4
----------------


======================================================
PA4 IMPLEMENTATION REPORT
======================================================

1. OVERVIEW

This project implements the semantic analysis phase of the
COOL compiler in C++.

The analyzer checks class declarations, inheritance,
attributes, methods, scopes, type conformance, and
expression typing.

Valid programs produce a type-annotated abstract syntax
tree (AST). Invalid programs produce semantic diagnostics
and are rejected.


2. ARCHITECTURE

The implementation is organized around ClassTable and
the infer_expression_type function.

ClassTable maintains information about the class
hierarchy, attributes, and methods.

It provides operations for:

- Class and type lookup.
- Inheritance validation.
- Method lookup through the inheritance hierarchy.
- Type conformance.
- Least upper bound computation.
- Attribute environment construction.
- Semantic error reporting.

The analysis is divided into two main stages.

First, class declarations and their relationships are
validated.

Second, expressions are analyzed in their appropriate
typing environments.

This separation prevents invalid class declarations from
compromising expression-level semantic analysis.


3. CLASS AND METHOD VALIDATION

The analyzer validates:

- Duplicate class declarations.
- Undefined parent classes.
- Forbidden inheritance relationships.
- Direct and indirect inheritance cycles.
- Duplicate attributes and methods.
- Illegal attribute redefinitions through inheritance.
- Method overriding signatures.
- Undefined declared types.
- Formal parameter declarations.
- Restrictions on the identifier self.
- The existence and signature of Main.main.

Inherited attributes and methods are resolved through
the class hierarchy.


4. TYPE INFERENCE AND SCOPES

Expression typing is performed recursively.

The implementation handles the COOL expression forms,
including:

- Arithmetic, comparison, and logical operations.
- Assignment and identifier lookup.
- Conditional and loop expressions.
- Blocks and local let declarations.
- Case expressions.
- Object creation and isvoid.
- Dynamic and static method dispatch.
- Constants and empty expressions.

Scoped symbol tables represent local environments.

Method parameters, inherited attributes, let variables,
and case branch variables are handled according to their
visibility and scoping rules.

The type of each successfully analyzed expression is
stored in its AST node.


5. INHERITANCE AND SELF_TYPE

Type conformance is checked by traversing the inheritance
hierarchy.

SELF_TYPE receives special treatment because its meaning
depends on the current class and expression context.

The least upper bound operation computes the common
ancestor required for conditional and case expressions.

Dynamic and static dispatch also account for methods
whose declared return type is SELF_TYPE.


6. ERROR HANDLING AND RECOVERY

Semantic errors are reported with source locations.

The implementation attempts to continue analyzing
independent expressions after recoverable errors.

An internal error-type marker helps prevent cascading
diagnostics caused by previously reported invalid types.

This marker is used for recovery only. It does not make
invalid COOL programs acceptable.

Independent errors remain detectable even when another
expression has already failed semantic analysis.


7. TESTING STRATEGY

The implementation was tested using 106 individual COOL
programs covering both valid and invalid semantics.

For valid programs, the type-annotated AST generated by
our analyzer was compared with the reference analyzer.

For invalid programs, rejection decisions and diagnostic
output were compared.

Results of the 106-test regression:

- 40 valid programs with identical AST output.
- 66 invalid programs rejected by both analyzers.
- 0 differences in acceptance or rejection.
- 0 differences in diagnostic output.
- 0 execution timeouts.

Additional integrated programs were prepared:

good.cl:
Exercises inheritance, method overriding, SELF_TYPE,
attributes, scopes, control flow, case expressions,
operators, and method dispatch.

bad.cl:
Exercises 14 independent semantic error scenarios,
including invalid method calls, incompatible types,
undefined identifiers, invalid operators, and illegal
assignments.

The integrated good.cl program produced an AST identical
to the reference implementation.

The integrated bad.cl program produced all 14 expected
diagnostics, identical to the reference implementation.

These results provide evidence of correctness for the
tested cases, although they do not constitute a formal
proof for every possible COOL program.


8. BUILD AND EXECUTION

The project uses the official PA4 Makefile and the
course-provided COOL compilation environment.

Build the semantic analyzer with:

    make semant

Run the analyzer through the compiler pipeline:

    ./lexer good.cl | ./parser | ./semant

The mysemant executable is the course-provided reference
implementation used for comparison.

The implementation follows the existing C++ interfaces
and does not require modifying the official Makefile.

The 106 individual regression tests are stored in
PA4/tests/. These files are organized separately from
the official submission files.

======================================================
END OF IMPLEMENTATION REPORT
======================================================
